# Capstone 3 – Blog Web Application

## 📌 Project Overview

Capstone 3 is a basic blog web application developed as part of my full-stack web development learning journey.

The application allows users to create, view, edit, and delete blog posts through a web interface. It also incorporates current date and time functionality.

Blog posts are temporarily stored while the application is running and are not saved permanently. Restarting the server clears the posts.

## 🛠️ Technologies Used

- **HTML5** – Structures the blog pages and content.
- **CSS3** – Provides styling and layout.
- **JavaScript** – Handles application logic and date/time functionality.
- **Node.js** – Runs JavaScript on the server.
- **Express.js** – Handles HTTP requests, routing, and form submissions.
- **EJS** – Dynamically renders blog posts and other content.
- **HTTP GET & POST** – Supports page requests and blog operations.

## ✨ Features

- **Create Posts** – Add new blog entries.
- **View Posts** – Display existing blog entries.
- **Edit Posts** – Modify previously created posts.
- **Delete Posts** – Remove blog entries.
- **Current Date & Time** – Incorporates dynamically generated date and time information.
- **Dynamic Content** – Displays blog content using server-side rendering.
- **Express Routing** – Uses `app.get()` and `app.post()` to process requests.
- **Temporary Storage** – Blog posts are not permanently saved.

## ⚙️ How It Works

The application uses Node.js and Express.js to manage requests and blog functionality.

- `app.get()` retrieves and displays pages.
- `app.post()` processes form submissions for creating, editing, and deleting posts.
- `app.listen()` starts the server on a configured port.
- EJS templates render dynamic blog content.
- JavaScript date and time functionality provides current date/time information.

## 💻 Running the Project Locally

### 1. Clone the repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lydiaperdomo/FullDev.git
```

### 2. Navigate to the project folder

```bash
cd FullDev/Capstone3
```

### 3. Install dependencies

```bash
npm install
```

### 4. Start the server

```bash
node index.js
```

Replace `index.js` if your server file has a different name.

### 5. Open the application

Visit `http://localhost:3000` if your application uses port 3000.

## ⚠️ Data Storage Limitations

This application does not use permanent database storage.

Posts are maintained temporarily in server memory and are lost when the application restarts.

## 🎯 Learning Objectives

- Develop server-side applications using Node.js and Express.js
- Implement CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations
- Handle HTTP GET and POST requests
- Use EJS for dynamic page rendering
- Process user input through HTML forms
- Work with JavaScript date and time functionality
- Understand temporary in-memory data storage
- Run applications using a local development server

## 📂 Project Type

**Full-Stack Web Development | Node.js, Express.js, EJS & JavaScript**

